<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Zentic itself is not sandboxed, deliberately. A browser reads and writes
  wherever the user points it - downloads to any folder, uploads from anywhere,
  files opened from Finder - and the App Sandbox turns each of those into a
  per-file permission dance. Browsers distributed outside the App Store (Chrome,
  Firefox, Arc) ship unsandboxed for the same reason. The part that runs
  untrusted code, web content, is still sandboxed: WebKit runs every page in its
  own sandboxed WebContent process whatever the host app does. Hardened Runtime
  (on in project.yml) and notarization protect the app process itself.

  Without the sandbox, the network and file entries below are not enforced. They
  are kept so the bundle states what it uses, and so turning the sandbox on later
  is a one-line change rather than an audit. The camera, microphone and location
  entries ARE enforced: Hardened Runtime blocks those devices without them.

  Tab sync's iCloud entries are in Zentic-iCloud.entitlements, used when
  ZENTIC_ICLOUD=YES: they need a provisioning profile, and this build must not.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-write</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
